    "result_text": "The full suite is green: **66 tests, 0 failures, 0 errors**.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented the `capacityWarning` flag following the exact pattern already used for the sibling `bulkSignupWarning` flag.\n\n**The rule:** on owner create, `capacityWarning` is `true` when the owner's city already holds between 40 and 49 owners (inclusive) \u2014 approaching the hard capacity limit of 50 \u2014 and `false` otherwise. The existing hard rejection at 50 (`CityAtCapacityException` \u2192 409) is untouched.\n\n**Changes made:**\n- `OwnerRestControllerV1.java` \u2014 added `CITY_CAPACITY_WARNING_THRESHOLD = 40` and a `capacityWarningFor(city)` helper that counts owners in the city case-insensitively (same counting logic as `rejectCityAtCapacity`) and returns `count >= 40 && count < CITY_CAPACITY`. It's computed and set on the owner in `addOwner`, right after the existing `bulkSignupWarning` assignment.\n- `model/Owner.java` \u2014 added the `capacityWarning` field (mapped to a `capacity_warning` column) plus getter/setter.\n- `openapi.yml` \u2014 added the read-only `capacityWarning` boolean property to the owner schema, so the generated `OwnerDto` carries it and MapStruct maps it by name.\n- Schema DDL (`hsqldb`, `h2`, `mysql`, `postgres`) \u2014 added the `capacity_warning` column so the value persists and is returned on read.\n\nThe new `Cp54Tests` (warns at 40\u219241) and the existing `Cp18Tests` (hard limit at 50) both pass, along with the entire suite.",
